  <s:String x:Key="Set.ClientOut">Where to pack client PBOs</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.ClientOutHint">The @mod folder that the server and players load with -mod=, for example C:\DayZServer\@MyAddon. The PBO and signature go into addons\, the .bikey into keys\. One folder can collect all client mods.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.ServerOut">Where to pack server PBOs</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.ServerOutHint">A separate folder for server mods, for example C:\DayZServer\@ServerMods. It is loaded with -serverMod=; players do not need it. If you give the server folder itself, the mod gets its own @folder in it, not the game's addons.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.GraftedOut">Grafted method: server mod folder</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.GraftedOutHint">The GRAFT README layout: @MOD\addons\MOD.pbo and @MOD\grafted\MOD.grafted.dll. The hid.dll host finds the plugin through -mod= or -serverMod= with this folder.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.OutEmpty">Empty field: a Grafted mod takes the server folder; if that is empty too, a GRAFT project uses dist\@Name inside the project and an ordinary mod @Name beside its folder. A common folder can also be set in a mod's build options (“Common folder…”), and so can a mod's own folder.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.Rapify">Binarize like pboProject</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.RapifyHint">config.cpp → config.bin, rvmats and p3d models (MLOD → ODOL) through CfgConvert and binarize.exe. Without it the folder is packed as it is.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.CheckRefs">Check file references</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Set.CheckRefsHint">Before packing, every texture, model, rvmat, sound and script folder named by config.cpp, rvmats, models, layouts and scripts must exist in the mod, on P:\ or in the game data (DZ). A missing file stops the pack with a list.</s:String>
